<description>
Fill holes inside a large template frame using shape-matched outside sprites.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color. One large monochrome frame object (template).
- Inside the frame: holes of various shapes (non-frame-color regions).
- Outside the frame: small monochrome sprites scattered on bg.

Rule:
- Crop to the frame's bounding box; fill entirely with frame color.
- For each hole, find an outside sprite whose shape matches (rotation/flip allowed).
- Fill the hole with the matched sprite's color. If multiple sprites match, use the most frequent color.

Key insight: Sprites act as shape-coded color keys — their shape identifies which hole they fill, their color determines the fill color.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_to_match",
  "src": "outside monochrome sprites s (connected components) on the background",
  "dst": "fill holes d (4-connected components of non-frame pixels) inside the largest frame sprite; output is cropped to the frame bbox",
  "match_cond": "s.shape == d.shape (allow rotation/flip); if multiple s match, prefer the most frequent s.color"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  task_type: move_to_match (shape-coded color keys fill template holes)
  grid: 30 rows x 30 cols [observed]
  bg: uniform fill of one color (most common in grid)

  frame (dst, the template):
    count: exactly 1 large monochrome rectangular region
    color: frame_col, distinct from bg
    bbox: 7-13 rows x 7-30 cols; area (bbox_area) = 80-220
    placement: inside grid; may touch grid edge; leaves open outside region for sprites
    interior: only frame_col and bg cells; bbox border is all frame_col
    symmetry: LR + UD + rot180 symmetric binary pattern (frame_col/bg); rot90 symmetric iff square [observed]
    frame_color_density: 0.65-0.80 of bbox cells are frame_col

  holes (non-frame 4-connected components inside frame bbox):
    count: 9-15; each bbox fits within ~1-5 cells in each dim
    unique_shape_count: 3-6 distinct shapes (under rotation/flip canonicalization)
    shape_bbox: 1x1 to 5x6; area 1-8 cells
    arrangement: holes positioned so the overall frame pattern has LR+UD+rot180 symmetry; each hole's bg cells are replaced by frame_col on the bbox border
    per_shape_multiplicity: 1-5 holes share each canonical shape; every hole shape matches at least one outside sprite (else filled with frame_col)

  sprites (src, outside frame bbox, on bg):
    count: 15-30 connected components, 8-connected, typically monochromatic (allow rare multi-color distractor)
    color: any non-bg, non-frame_col color; 6-7 distinct sprite colors per grid
    placement: fully outside frame bbox, separated from other sprites and from frame bbox by >=1 bg cell (Chebyshev)
    shape_bbox: 1x1 to 5x6; area 1-8 cells (matches hole shape ranges)
    composition: for each hole shape, provide 2-13 sprite instances in varied colors; the MOST FREQUENT color among shape-matching sprites determines the fill color (ties -> smaller color id); 0-1 distractor shapes (no matching hole)

  output:
    shape: equal to frame bbox (th x tw)
    content: start from solid frame_col; for each hole, fill the hole mask cells with the chosen matched color
    symmetry: LR + UD + rot180 (inherited from symmetric hole layout + symmetric key assignment)

generation_strategy: |
  Output-first construction, then invert to build input.

  1. Pick grid = 30x30, bg (random color), frame_col (random color != bg).
  2. Choose frame bbox dims th x tw in [7..13] x [7..30]; place bbox inside grid (optionally touching one edge).
  3. Build a SYMMETRIC hole layout inside a blank th x tw canvas:
     a. Start with a canvas full of frame_col.
     b. Choose K_shapes = 3-6 distinct canonical hole shapes (each is a small bool mask with bbox <=5x6, area 1-8). Use simple library: rectangles 1x1,1x2,2x2,2x3,3x3, plus/cross/L/T variants.
     c. For each chosen shape, decide multiplicity 1-5. Randomly place each hole instance on the canvas so the FULL layout is LR+UD+rot180 symmetric: place at one position, then mirror across both axes (dedup if centered).
     d. Ensure: holes are 4-disconnected from each other (surround each with frame_col cells); holes do not touch the bbox border (border stays frame_col); frame-color fraction ends up ~0.65-0.80.
  4. The assembled canvas IS the output grid. Record (hole_shape_canonical -> chosen_color) mapping by assigning each hole-shape a distinct sprite_color drawn from palette minus {bg, frame_col}.
  5. Derive input:
     a. Copy output canvas into input grid at chosen frame bbox position; replace each hole cell (non-frame_col) with bg to get the "hole appears as bg" input frame.
     b. Fill rest of input grid with bg.
     c. For each hole-shape with assigned color C_h and multiplicity m_h: scatter N_sprites (N_sprites = m_h + random extras, so chosen C_h is the plurality) sprite instances with that shape (optionally rotated/flipped) in color C_h; also scatter some other-color sprite instances of the same shape (in non-winning colors) so C_h is the MOST FREQUENT color for this shape.
     d. Optionally add 0-1 distractor sprites of a shape not matching any hole.
     e. Place all sprites outside the frame bbox, each with >=1 bg-cell separation from each other and from the frame bbox (Chebyshev). Reject-and-retry on collision.
  6. Validate with solver: solver(input) must equal the target output canvas.
```
